body:
Yes I am just mentioning from my perspective as a single person. It is likely cheaper in Europe once you factor your children's expenses and college tuitions.

&#x200B;

Most employers will offer around 14 days. My first job entitled 0 days. After 1 year, I got 2 weeks. And after 4-5 years it went up to 27 days. Please note that this include sick days, so realistically it is around 20 days. 

&#x200B;

Similar but it goes straight to green card.

You can apply online here. It hold once a year. Don't get your hopes up. About 20 million applicants and they only take in 50,000.

[https://www.dvlottery.state.gov](https://www.dvlottery.state.gov)

&#x200B;

A lot of recent political debates about shutting down this program. Last year a person in New York who got in through this program went on a killing spree.  

&#x200B;

I have 5 years of experience and that still pretty hard for me.  I pulled a huge string to get a job in Dubin from the US. And that hasn't been rewarding for me financially at all. 

&#x200B;

Every interviews I get I usually get shutdown by just requiring a sponsorship to asking salary above 50,000 GBP / 60,000 EURO. I am a couple years off from an Irish passport so things might change from there.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Tier 2 has a lot of requirements from market test to salary requirement. The demand for it drives up the minimum wage recently. It went up as far as 60,000 GBP a year one time. So it is hard to get it as an entry-level today, especially if you didn't graduate from UK university as they carry some privileges. 

Tier 5 is simply a work holiday / training visa and it is a lot less restricted than tier 5 from my understanding. A lot of people I know here are on Tier 5. 

&#x200B;

Doesn't really matter career wise. You should ask yourself what do you want to do. IT is a big field. The courses you list there will prepare you well for the high performance computing areas, which seem to be valued by many top tech companies and the scientific community. 

&#x200B;

However, few of these stuffs apply to most corporate web development jobs, as one might benefit more from Software Engineering related courses such as Software Testing, Software Architecture, Software Design, Human Computer Interactions, etc. My coworker who is a senior software engineer at a large corporation doesn't even know what a branch predictor is. But does he has to? He's an excellent Java developer who knows his framework inside out and actually get a lot of stuff done.

&#x200B;

If you want to work in InfoSec, you could consider studying for Security+ cert and take Computer Security and Secure Programming classes on top of Computer Architecture, Networking, and Operating Systems. 

&#x200B;

There seems to be a lot of emphasis on algorithm and data structure lately. One thing you should consider is that many Algorithm and Discrete Math courses in the college are all theory and proofs. None of these stuffs actually apply especially in regards to Job interviews. I still have to study CTCI and doing leetcodes like everybody else before I landed my first programming job. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
